  "answer_text": "The accepted and \"official\" pronunciation of **doge** (as in the Shiba Inu meme and the cryptocurrency Dogecoin) is **\"dohj\"**. \n\n### How to Say It\n* **Phonetically:** /do\u028ad\u0292/ or /do\u028a\u0292/\n* **Sound-alike:** Pronounce it like the **\"doh\"** in *Play-Doh* (or Homer Simpson's \"D'oh!\"), followed immediately by a soft **\"j\"** sound (as in *judge*) or a **\"zh\"** sound (like the \"s\" in *treasure* or *measure*). \n* **One Syllable:** It is a single syllable that sounds somewhat like \"doje\".\n\n---\n\n### Why is this the \"official\" pronunciation?\nThe spelling \"D-O-G-E\" as an intentional misspelling of \"dog\" traces back to a June 2005 episode of the puppet web series ***Homestar Runner*** (specifically a short called \"Biz Cas Fri 1\"). \n\nIn the episode, Homestar Runner refers to Strong Bad as his \"D-O-G-E\" and pronounces it **\"dohj\"**. Because this is the exact origin of the internet slang term, this pronunciation became the default standard. \n\nJackson Palmer, the co-creator of **Dogecoin**, also officially confirmed that they pronounce the cryptocurrency as **\"dohj-coin\"** specifically because of this *Homestar Runner* video.\n\n---\n\n### Common (But Technically Incorrect) Alternatives\nBecause the word was primarily read on the internet for years before being spoken aloud, several other pronunciations became popular:\n\n1. **\"Dohg\"** (Rhymes with *vogue* or *rogue*): Many people naturally assumed the \"g\" was hard because it is a misspelling of \"dog\".\n2. **\"Doh-gay\" or \"Doh-jeh\"** (Two syllables): Because the Shiba Inu is a Japanese dog breed, many assumed the word had Japanese roots and pronounced it with a Japanese phonetic style. Additionally, the historical Italian title *Doge* (referring to the former rulers of Venice) is pronounced this way. \n3. **\"Dodge\"** (Rhymes with *lodge*): Some people simply read the letters and pronounced it like the car brand or the dodgeball game.\n4. **\"Dog-ee\"** (Like *doggy*): Believed by some to be a stylized spelling of \"doggy.\"",
